    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **36 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in the owner create flow.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.toE164(String raw)` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\nAfter the existing generic \"8\u201315 digits\" check, I added a country-specific length check on the assembled E.164 digit string:\n- `+61` (Australia) \u2192 requires exactly **9** national digits\n- `+1` (NANP) \u2192 requires exactly **10** national digits\n- any other country code \u2192 unchanged (generic 8\u201315 rule)\n\nWhen the national length is wrong for the country, `toE164` returns `null`, which causes `normalizeTelephone` to throw `RequiredFieldsMissingException` \u2192 **HTTP 400**, exactly as the new `Cp26Tests` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) requires.\n\n**Why this placement is safe:**\n- `toE164` is the single normalization point used both for the submitted number and for comparing stored owners during duplicate detection; the duplicate check already filters out `null` results, so no regression there.\n- The prefix detection is unambiguous for the two specified codes \u2014 Australian numbers start `61\u2026`, NANP numbers start `1\u2026`, and other countries (e.g. the `+64` case in `Cp08Tests`) fall through to the generic rule and are still accepted.\n- The valid AU numbers used throughout the test fixtures (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits) remain valid.\n\nI updated the `toE164` Javadoc to document the new per-country rule.",
